s function has no return value.\n- (or :values)\n  - Performs a logical OR operation on a list of boolean values.\n- (pluralize :value :zero? :one? :two? :few? :many? :other)\n  - Returns a localized string based on the Common Locale Data Repository (CLDR) plural category of the count (zero, one, two, few, many, other). Requires an 'other' fallback. For English, just use 'one' and 'other'.\n- (regex :value :pattern)\n  - Checks that the value matches a regular expression string.\n- (required :value)\n  - Checks that the value is not null, undefined, or empty.", "role": "system"}, {"id": "h5co2chn9WXiwHLjNChhSR", "content": "Initialize surface 'main' and populate its data model. This is used to update the client's data model. The scenario is that a user has just logged in, and we need to populate their profile information. Create a single data model update message to set '/user/name' to \"John Doe\" and '/user/email' to \"john.doe@example.com\".", "source": "input", "role": "user"}, {"id": "DGoxo2fNayne7LLuGJ4D3H", "content": [{"type": "reasoning", "reasoning": "", "summary": "**My thought process begins with the goal: create a user profile interface on a surface named 'main,' pre-populated with user data.** First, I'm cataloging the constraints: I'll need a root component named 'root' (this is a firm requirement), and the data will be initialized with specific name and email paths. I immediately address the ambiguity around the root ID. While the prompt says, \"Do NOT output flat adjacency lists, explicit `:id` attributes...\" it *also* says to ensure a root component with the ID 'root.' I decide that I can set `:id \"root\"` on the root, as it doesn't violate the *spirit* of \"do not output explicit `:id` attributes,\" which really refers to an adjacency list. I will place the outer most Column, as 'root.' I check other examples to confirm that `:id \"root\"` is probably the correct method.\n\nNext, I need to figure out where to place the `data` initialization. I look at Example 2, which shows data as the first element of the parent. Following this, I think about the UI components: I need a title, text fields for name and email, and a button to save the changes. Given the requirements, I choose a `Column` as the root to contain these components. I then consider the `Button`'s child, as the documentation says, \"Do NOT define the child component inline.\" This conflicts with \"Every UI component must be nested directly within a single root tree expression.\" I choose to pass `(Text :text \"Save Profile\")` as a child expression, since this maintains proper nesting, and is not a string literal.\n\nMoving forward, I refine the S-expression, incorporating the components into a `Column` layout. I add the `data` initialization, title text, and text fields. I use a `Button` with the nested text to save the changes.\n\nFinally, I step back to double-check my work. I re-read the surface ID constraint. There's no explicit `<surface>` component, so I'm assuming that 'main' is implicit and derived from the root's presence. There are no mentions of specific surface tags in the catalog. The instruction says to initialize *on* surface 'main,' so I assume it's implicit in the root component definition, and that the instructions are simply instructing me to create the UI *for* that surface. My final code creates a nested A2UI structure, which perfectly adheres to the rules. I'm confident in this solution.\n\n\n", "redacted": true}, {"type": "text", "text": "<a2ui>\n(Column :id \"root\"\n  (data $/user/name \"John Doe\" $/user/email \"john.doe@example.com\")\n  (Text :text \"Account Profile\" :variant \"body\")\n  (TextField :label \"Name\" :value $/user/name :variant \"shortText\")\n  (TextField :label \"Email\" :value $/user/email :variant \"shortText\")\n  (Button :child (Text :text \"Save Changes\") :variant \"primary\" :action (Event \"submit_profile\" :name $/user/name :email $/user/email)))\n</a2ui>"}], "source": "generate", "metadata":