scaleDown'\n  - :variant: A hint for the image size and style. Must be one of: 'icon', 'avatar', 'smallFeature', 'mediumFeature', 'largeFeature', 'header'\n  - :weight: The relative weight of this component within a Row or Column. This is similar to the CSS 'flex-grow' property. Note: this may ONLY be set when the component is a direct descendant of a Row or Column.\n- (List :children :direction? :align? :weight?)\n  - :children: Defines the children. Use an array of strings for a fixed set of children, or a template object to generate children from a data list.\n  - :direction: The direction in which the list items are laid out. Must be one of: 'vertical', 'horizontal'\n  - :align: Defines the alignment of children along the cross axis. Must be one of: 'start', 'center', 'end', 'stretch'\n  - :weight: The relative weight of this component within a Row or Column. This is similar to the CSS 'flex-grow' property. Note: this may ONLY be set when the component is a direct descendant of a Row or Column.\n- (Modal :trigger :content :weight?)\n  - :trigger: The ID of the component that opens the modal when interacted with (e.g., a button). Do NOT define the component inline.\n  - :content: The ID of the component to be displayed inside the modal. Do NOT define the component inline.\n  - :weight: The relative weight of this component within a Row or Column. This is similar to the CSS 'flex-grow' property. Note: this may ONLY be set when the component is a direct descendant of a Row or Column.\n- (Row :children :justify? :align? :weight?)\n  - A layout component that arranges its children horizontally. To create a grid layout, nest Columns within this Row.\n  - :children: Defines the children. Use an array of strings for a fixed set of children, or a template object to generate children from a data list. Children cannot be defined inline, they must be referred to by ID.\n  - :justify: Defines the arrangement of children along the main axis (horizontally). Use 'spaceBetween' to push items to the edges, or 'start'/'end'/'center' to pack them together. Must be one of: 'center', 'end', 'spaceAround', 'spaceBetween', 'spaceEvenly', 'start', 'stretch'\n  - :align: Defines the alignment of children along the cross axis (vertically). This is similar to the CSS 'align-items' property, but uses camelCase values (e.g., 'start'). Must be one of: 'start', 'center', 'end', 'stretch'\n  - :weight: The relative weight of this component within a Row or Column. This is similar to the CSS 'flex-grow' property. Note: this may ONLY be set when the component is a direct descendant of a Row or Column.\n- (Slider :label? :min? :max :value :steps? :weight? :checks?)\n  - :label: The label for the slider.\n  - :min: The minimum value of the slider.\n  - :max: The maximum value of the slider.\n  - :value: The current value of the slider.\n  - :steps: The number of discrete divisions in the slider range. If specified, the slider will snap to discrete values.\n  - :weight: The relative weight of this component within a Row or Column. This is similar to the CSS 'flex-grow' property. Note: this may ONLY be set when the component is a direct descendant of a Row or Column.\n- (Tabs :tabs :weight?)\n  - :tabs: An array of objects, where each object defines a tab with a title and a child component.\n  - :weight: The relative weight of this component within a Row or Column. This is similar to the CSS 'flex-grow' property. Note: this may ONLY be set when the component is a direct descendant of a Row or Column.\n- (Text :text :variant? :weight?)\n  - :text: The text content to display. While simple Markdown formatting is supported (i.e. without HTML, images, or links), utilizing dedicated UI components is generally preferred for a richer and more structured presentation.\n  - :variant: A hint for the base text style. Must be one of: 'caption', 'body'\n  - :weight: The relative weight of this component within a Row or Column. This is similar to the CSS 'flex-grow' property. Note: this may ONLY be set when the component is a direct descendant of a Row or Column.\n- (TextField :label :value? :placeholder? :variant? :weight? :checks?)\n  - :label: The text label for the input field.\n  - :value: The value of the text field.\n  - :placeholder: The placeholder text for the input field.\n  - :variant: The type of input field to display. Must be one of: 'longText', 'number', 'shortText', 'obscured'\n  - :weight: The relative weight of this component within a Row or Column. This is similar to the CSS 'flex-grow' property. Note: this may ONLY be set when the component is a direct descendant of a Row or Column.\n- (Video :url :posterUrl? :weight?)\n  - :url: The URL of the video to display.\n  - :posterUrl: The URL of the poster image to display before the video plays.\n  - :weight: The relative weight of this component within a Row or Column. This is similar to the CSS 'flex-grow' property. Note: this may ONLY be set when the component is a direct descendant of a Row or Column.\n\n## Function Signatures:\n- (and :values)\n  - Performs a logical AND operation on a list of boolean values.\n- (email :value)\n  - Checks that the value is a valid email address.\n- (formatCurrency :value :currency :decimals? :grouping?)\n  - Formats a number as a currency string.\n- (formatDate :value :format)\n  - Formats a timestamp into a string using a pattern.\n- (formatNumber :value :decimals? :grouping?)\n  - Formats a number with the specified grouping and decimal precision.\n- (formatString :value)\n  - Performs string interpolation of data model values and other functions in the catalog functions list and returns the resulting string. The value string can contain interpolated expressions in the `${expression}` format. Supported expression types include: JSON Pointer paths to the data model (e.g., `${/absolute/path}` or `${relative/path}`), and client-side function calls (e.g., `${now()}`). Function arguments must be named (e.g., `${formatDate(value:${/currentDate}, format:'MM-dd')}`). To include a literal `${` sequence, escape it as `\\${`.\n- (length :value :min? :max?)\n  - Checks string length constraints.\n- (not :value)\n  - Performs a logical NOT operation on a boolean value.\n- (numeric :value :min? :max?)\n  - Checks numeric range constraints.\n- (openUrl :url)\n  - Opens the specified URL in a browser or handler. This function has no return value.\n- (or :values)\n  - Performs a logical OR operation on a list of boolean values.\n- (pluralize :value :zero? :one? :two? :few? :many? :other)\n  - Returns a localized string based on the Common Locale Data Repository (CLDR) plural category of the count (zero, one, two, few, many, other). Requires an 'other' fallback. For English, just use 'one' and 'other'.\n- (regex :value :pattern)\n  - Checks that the value matches a regular expression string.\n- (required :value)\n  - Checks that the value is not null, undefined, or empty.",
-        "e34a046c1a0d452a5aa1929965af4002": "**Alright, let's break down this task step-by-step to create the user settings UI according to these A2UI guidelines.**\n\nFirst, the objective is crystal clear: build a User Settings UI on the 'main' surface, and the UI will be anchored by a root component with the ID 'root'. This means the entire S-expression structure will be wrapped in that root component. I have to create this page with tabs, one for profile and the other for notifications, alongside a modal for deleting an account. The key here is to stick to the A2UI Atom Grammar, use the given components, and ensure inline component declarations where they are allowed and explicit identifier references for others.\n\nI'm initially concerned because the instructions on component definitions appear contradictory, especially regarding whether to reference child components by ID or inline. I've re-read the rules multiple times and I must directly nest components and child components without using `:id` attributes and flat lists, the apparent goal is the structure the components in a tree. After reviewing the documentation, I'm confident that the A2UI syntax requires direct nesting despite the catalog descriptions, especially because of the constraint: \"Do NOT output flat adjacency lists, explicit :id attributes, or separate component variable IDs. Every UI component must be nested directly within a single root tree expression.\" So the rules seem to indicate that I can't reference child components by ID. I can only nest them inline as children of the parent component.\n\nThe user interface will need a `Tabs` component, composed of two tabs: \"Profile\" and \"# Notifications\". The Profile tab will contain a `Column` with a `TextField` for the user's name, while the Notifications tab will include a `Column` with a `CheckBox` to enable email notifications. A `Modal` component, triggered by a \"Delete Account\" button, will present a confirmation dialog. It is imperative that the A2UI syntax guidelines are followed.\n\nThe components need to be placed directly within parent container expressions and nested as children or sequential positional arguments based on the standard Lisp/S-expression conventions.\n\nThe plan is to structure the page as follows:\n\n*   The root will be a `Column` with the `id` \"root\".\n*   Within the root, I'll include data initialization using the `(data ...)` function.\n*   I will nest the main page elements directly within it.\n*   Let's add a \"Account Settings\" header text with `(Text :text \"Account Settings\")` so it looks like a Settings page.\n*   Then, I'll include the `Tabs` component for the user settings in the form of `[(:title \"Profile\" :child (Column ...)) ...]` and use a `Column` for the Profile and Notifications.\n*   The Profile tab will contain a `TextField` with a label \"Name\" and a value bound to `$/profile/name`.\n*   The Notifications tab will have a `CheckBox` for \"Enable email notifications\", with a value tied to `$/notifications/emailEnabled`.\n*   A `Modal` component will be added that's triggered by a \"Delete Account\" button and contains a confirmation message and \"Confirm Deletion\" and \"Cancel\" buttons. The `Modal` and components nested directly as children of the `Column` with trigger and content properly specified and nested. The `Event` expressions will be added to trigger the functionality (e.g. `(Event \"click_delete\")`).\n\nI'll finalize it by organizing a root level that contains the data (data model initialization), a header title text (Account Settings), a tab, a divider, and a modal for the account deletion. The UI output will be written as a valid, well-structured S-expression.\n\nI need to initialize the data model as well, specifically the `$/profile/name` and `$/notifications/emailEnabled` state variables using `(data ...)` at the beginning of the root `Column`.\n\nI will be careful with the S-expression format to ensure the UI tree is clean, readable, well-nested, and strictly adheres to A2UI syntax.\n\n\n",
-        "6e5858fc6bfcd1c8deedb3af974244fb": "<a2ui>\n(Column :id \"root\"\n  (data $/profile/name \"John Doe\" $/notifications/emailEnabled true)\n  (Text :text \"Account Settings\")\n  (Tabs :tabs [\n    (:title \"Profile\" :child (Column (TextField :label \"Name\" :value $/profile/name)))\n    (:title \"# Notifications\" :child (Column (CheckBox :label \"Enable email notifications\" :value $/notifications/emailEnabled)))\n  ])\n  (Divider :axis \"horizontal\")\n  (Modal\n    :trigger (Button :variant \"primary\" :action (Event \"show_delete_modal\") :child (Text :text \"Delete Account\"))\n    :content (Column\n      (Text :text \"Are you sure you want to delete your account? This action cannot be undone.\")\n      (Row\n        (Button :variant \"primary\" :action (Event \"confirm_delete\") :child (Text :text \"Confirm Deletion\"))\n        (Button :variant \"borderless\" :action (Event \"cancel_delete\") :child (Text :text \"Cancel\"))\n      )\n    )\n  )\n)\n</a2ui>",
